WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:01.357
- Good morning, Believe Nation.

00:00:01.357 --> 00:00:04.442
My name is Evan Carmichael,
my one word is believe,

00:00:04.442 --> 00:00:06.791
and I believe that
entrepreneurs are going to solve

00:00:06.791 --> 00:00:09.598
all of the world's major problems.

00:00:09.598 --> 00:00:11.102
So to help you on your journey today,

00:00:11.102 --> 00:00:14.122
we're going to learn how to get great.

00:00:14.122 --> 00:00:16.082
Over to you, Dean Graziosi.

00:00:16.082 --> 00:00:18.469
(rooster crows)

00:00:18.469 --> 00:00:20.973
♫ I wake up every mornin'

00:00:20.973 --> 00:00:23.174
♫ Espresso keep me goin'

00:00:23.174 --> 00:00:26.674
♫ I wake up every mornin'

00:00:27.663 --> 00:00:29.761
- The biggest lie we've ever been told

00:00:29.761 --> 00:00:31.771
is to work on our weaknesses.

00:00:31.771 --> 00:00:32.789
Biggest BS in the world.

00:00:32.789 --> 00:00:34.278
The hell with your weaknesses,

00:00:34.278 --> 00:00:36.339
I was in special reading til 12th Grade,

00:00:36.339 --> 00:00:38.073
I couldn't spell for sh--.

00:00:38.073 --> 00:00:40.228
Right now, my computer
if it could talk to me,

00:00:40.228 --> 00:00:41.752
I try to type words and send an email

00:00:41.752 --> 00:00:43.300
and I'll go through three
different variations.

00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:44.892
It can't even find the word.

00:00:44.892 --> 00:00:47.452
It's like give up bro, use a simpler word.

00:00:47.452 --> 00:00:49.874
You just don't have the
vocabulary and I know that.

00:00:49.874 --> 00:00:51.622
But I still have multiple
New York Times bestsellers.

00:00:51.622 --> 00:00:52.563
I've been touched a lot,

00:00:52.563 --> 00:00:54.145
been able to touch the
lives of millions of people

00:00:54.145 --> 00:00:56.560
because here's the secret:

00:00:56.560 --> 00:00:59.020
get great at what you're already good at

00:00:59.020 --> 00:01:01.486
and pay people to do the
sh-- that you suck at.

00:01:01.486 --> 00:01:02.471
(crowd cheering)

00:01:02.471 --> 00:01:03.910
Does that make sense?

00:01:03.910 --> 00:01:05.382
Get great at what you're good at.

00:01:05.382 --> 00:01:06.362
I'm going to give you one example.

00:01:06.362 --> 00:01:07.630
Ned Hallowell.

00:01:07.630 --> 00:01:09.237
Oprah in America considers him

00:01:09.237 --> 00:01:11.603
the number one ADD
specialist in the world.

00:01:11.603 --> 00:01:13.447
Attention Deficit Disorder.

00:01:13.447 --> 00:01:15.003
I said to him one day,
we became good friends,

00:01:15.003 --> 00:01:16.950
I said, "How do you cure so many kids?"

00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:18.974
"Get 'em off Ritalin and
get 'em off medication."

00:01:18.974 --> 00:01:20.944
He said "Simple."

00:01:20.944 --> 00:01:23.600
He said "I find out what
happens as Johhny's in class.

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:24.697
"He's trying to read a book.

00:01:24.697 --> 00:01:26.771
"His teacher says, "You
sit there and read that

00:01:26.771 --> 00:01:28.424
"and you can't get up until you're done."

00:01:28.424 --> 00:01:31.722
"He freaks out, runs around,
now he's got ADHD, he's hyper."

00:01:31.722 --> 00:01:33.520
He said, "What we do, and
then they medicate him."

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:36.751
He goes, "What I do is I
find what Johnny's good at.

00:01:36.751 --> 00:01:39.626
"Maybe he's good at math, gym, soccer

00:01:39.626 --> 00:01:42.196
"and we get his team around
him, his family around him,

00:01:42.196 --> 00:01:44.590
"and we help him get great
at what he's good at.

00:01:44.590 --> 00:01:46.211
"His confidence goes up

00:01:46.211 --> 00:01:48.135
"and Johnny reads the book on his own."

00:01:48.135 --> 00:01:49.093
- I don't know why we're taught

00:01:49.093 --> 00:01:51.245
to focus on our weaknesses so much.

00:01:51.245 --> 00:01:54.256
The world belongs to the
people who are experts

00:01:54.256 --> 00:01:57.303
at what they do and to
provide insane value,

00:01:57.303 --> 00:02:00.668
not to the person who is
mediocre at everything.

00:02:00.668 --> 00:02:03.733
This a great story about
the bunny that forgot to hop

00:02:03.733 --> 00:02:05.128
and I forget who initially told it,

00:02:05.128 --> 00:02:07.162
but there's this bunny
who's going to school

00:02:07.162 --> 00:02:09.440
and he's learning to hop
and he's learning to swim.

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:12.289
He gets an A on his hopping but
he's failing in his swimming

00:02:12.289 --> 00:02:14.491
and so his parents start
freaking out and say,

00:02:14.491 --> 00:02:15.962
"Okay, we got to take you to swim lessons.

00:02:15.962 --> 00:02:16.832
"You got to get better at swimming.

00:02:16.832 --> 00:02:18.241
"You got to practice,
you got to hustle harder.

00:02:18.241 --> 00:02:19.924
"We're going to take
you to private lessons.

00:02:19.924 --> 00:02:21.078
"We're going to take you out to school

00:02:21.078 --> 00:02:23.178
"where you're going to learn,
learn, learn about how to swim."

00:02:23.178 --> 00:02:26.656
And slowly, slowly, slowly
he gets better to swim

00:02:26.656 --> 00:02:28.518
and he went from being
an F student in swimming

00:02:28.518 --> 00:02:30.482
to a C student in swimming.

00:02:30.482 --> 00:02:33.802
But because he spent so much time

00:02:33.802 --> 00:02:37.742
being a C student at swimming
and bridging that gap,

00:02:37.742 --> 00:02:40.605
he also falls from being
an A student in hopping

00:02:40.605 --> 00:02:42.592
to be a C student at hopping.

00:02:42.592 --> 00:02:46.813
And kind of along the way,
he forgets how to hop.

00:02:46.813 --> 00:02:51.214
And that's what a bunny
is supposed to do is hop.

00:02:51.214 --> 00:02:54.239
And so the world will tell you
to focus on your weaknesses.

00:02:54.239 --> 00:02:56.065
The world will tell you all
the things that you suck at

00:02:56.065 --> 00:02:57.640
and that you need to get better at.

00:02:57.640 --> 00:03:00.663
Understand that for you to
have the impact that you want,

00:03:00.663 --> 00:03:02.005
to make the money that you want,

00:03:02.005 --> 00:03:03.362
or to reach the people that you want,

00:03:03.362 --> 00:03:05.258
to build the business that you want,

00:03:05.258 --> 00:03:09.751
the best way to do it is to
not focus on your weaknesses.

00:03:09.751 --> 00:03:12.103
It's to find the things
that you are great at.

00:03:12.103 --> 00:03:13.919
Find the thing that you can be

00:03:13.919 --> 00:03:16.359
one of the best in the world at.

00:03:16.359 --> 00:03:18.180
You may not have that talent yet,

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:21.210
but you have the ability
to be one of the greatest

00:03:21.210 --> 00:03:25.491
in the world at doing
X, what is that thing?

00:03:25.491 --> 00:03:28.927
Focus as much time on
that thing as possible

00:03:28.927 --> 00:03:31.737
and then as you build and
grow, surround yourself

00:03:31.737 --> 00:03:34.291
with the people who
complement your weaknesses

00:03:34.291 --> 00:03:38.401
'cause they love doing
the things that you hate.

00:03:38.401 --> 00:03:40.170
That's how successful teams are built.

00:03:40.170 --> 00:03:42.195
That's how successful
businesses are built.

00:03:42.195 --> 00:03:45.372
Stop focusing on your weaknesses.

00:03:45.372 --> 00:03:47.181
So the question today is I'm curious,

00:03:47.181 --> 00:03:50.491
"What was the biggest
weakness that you gave up,

00:03:50.491 --> 00:03:52.601
"that people said you needed to focus on,

00:03:52.601 --> 00:03:55.014
"but you felt like I'm not
going to do this anymore.

00:03:55.014 --> 00:03:57.094
"I have more important, bigger things,

00:03:57.094 --> 00:03:58.738
"that I'm more talented at,
that I'm going to go out

00:03:58.738 --> 00:04:00.102
"and crush instead.

00:04:00.102 --> 00:04:02.374
"What was that biggest
weakness that you gave up

00:04:02.374 --> 00:04:03.957
"and handed to somebody else?"

00:04:03.957 --> 00:04:05.339
Leave it down in the comments below,

00:04:05.339 --> 00:04:06.520
really curious to find out.

00:04:06.520 --> 00:04:09.092
I also want to give a quick
shout out to Alejandro Rivas.

00:04:09.092 --> 00:04:10.749
Alejandro, thank you
so much for picking up

00:04:10.749 --> 00:04:12.593
a copy of my book, "Your One Word."

00:04:12.593 --> 00:04:14.422
It really, really,
really means a lot to me

00:04:14.422 --> 00:04:16.377
and I hope you're enjoying the read.

00:04:16.377 --> 00:04:17.687
So thank you guys again for watching.

00:04:17.687 --> 00:04:18.829
I believe in you.

00:04:18.829 --> 00:04:21.004
I hope we continue to believe in yourself

00:04:21.004 --> 00:04:23.174
and whatever your one word is.

00:04:23.174 --> 00:04:25.706
Much love and I'll see
you again tomorrow morning

00:04:25.706 --> 00:04:28.283
for another shot of Espresso.

00:04:28.283 --> 00:04:31.035
♫ I wake up every morning

00:04:31.035 --> 00:04:34.618
♫ Espresso keep me going

00:04:34.618 --> 00:04:36.969
(clacking)

00:04:36.969 --> 00:04:38.759
- You need to bet on your strengths

00:04:38.759 --> 00:04:41.631
and don't give a f--
about what you suck at.

00:04:41.631 --> 00:04:42.997
Way too many people in this room

00:04:42.997 --> 00:04:44.777
are going to spend the next
30, 40 years of their lives

00:04:44.777 --> 00:04:46.811
trying to check the boxes of the things

00:04:46.811 --> 00:04:48.764
that they're not as good
at and that you're going to

00:04:48.764 --> 00:04:50.956
waste a f-- load of time and lose.

00:04:50.956 --> 00:04:52.925
I highly recommend auditing yourself

00:04:52.925 --> 00:04:56.506
or if you have no f-- empathy or EQ

00:04:56.506 --> 00:04:58.493
or self-awareness, then
find somebody in your family

00:04:58.493 --> 00:05:01.697
or friendship that does and
let them tell you who you are.

00:05:01.697 --> 00:05:03.793
And once you believe
that, either for yourself

00:05:03.793 --> 00:05:06.016
or someone else told you, go directly,

00:05:06.016 --> 00:05:09.006
all chips all into that
because that is the only

00:05:09.006 --> 00:05:12.950
possible way, in my opinion
watching from the outside,

00:05:12.950 --> 00:05:14.421
that is, let me rephrase.

00:05:14.421 --> 00:05:18.351
That is a very highly,
likely way of over-indexing

00:05:18.351 --> 00:05:21.511
because the truth is, if
you want to be an anomaly,

00:05:21.511 --> 00:05:23.678
you've got to act like one.

00:05:24.733 --> 00:05:26.581
And so, that's it, that's what I got,

00:05:26.581 --> 00:05:28.845
so thanks for having me.

00:05:28.845 --> 00:05:31.508
- You need to know what
your unique strengths are

00:05:31.508 --> 00:05:33.286
and be willing to delegate them.

00:05:33.286 --> 00:05:35.761
So I would say that the
question of whether you should

00:05:35.761 --> 00:05:39.629
take a skill, learn it yourself,
delegate it to someone else

00:05:39.629 --> 00:05:42.886
or hire a new team
member, are determined by

00:05:42.886 --> 00:05:46.136
having a very accurate self-assessment,

00:05:47.230 --> 00:05:49.166
an assessment of your team skills,

00:05:49.166 --> 00:05:52.216
not only at what you can do,
but what you should be doing.

00:05:52.216 --> 00:05:55.069
That's very important, so
if you are a startup CEO

00:05:55.069 --> 00:05:59.236
or founder, and you have a
team of say two to 10 people,

00:06:00.164 --> 00:06:03.503
as soon as you have funds to hire,

00:06:03.503 --> 00:06:07.436
you should not be focused on
the things that you are bad at.

00:06:07.436 --> 00:06:09.938
Sounds very, very intuitive, but it's not

00:06:09.938 --> 00:06:12.423
because you might have enough
time to do those things,

00:06:12.423 --> 00:06:14.761
but the question is do you
have the attention to dedicate

00:06:14.761 --> 00:06:17.833
to those things so they're
not an interruption?

00:06:17.833 --> 00:06:19.945
Especially if you're
a venture-back startup

00:06:19.945 --> 00:06:21.454
and you're competing
against other companies

00:06:21.454 --> 00:06:23.340
that also have a lot of funding.

00:06:23.340 --> 00:06:25.996
The game and the race goes to the swift

00:06:25.996 --> 00:06:28.638
so you have to have a lot of velocity.

00:06:28.638 --> 00:06:31.664
- Knowing your strengths and weaknesses

00:06:31.664 --> 00:06:34.414
and knowing what you enjoy doing.

00:06:35.395 --> 00:06:38.640
If you look at it as a
job, you've already lost.

00:06:38.640 --> 00:06:39.934
It's not going to be your passion

00:06:39.934 --> 00:06:41.910
and you're going to count the hours.

00:06:41.910 --> 00:06:45.660
If you look at it as
something you love to do

00:06:47.076 --> 00:06:49.398
and you know what your strengths are,

00:06:49.398 --> 00:06:51.841
then you can leverage those strengths

00:06:51.841 --> 00:06:53.731
in your business and in helping others

00:06:53.731 --> 00:06:56.278
and once you recognize your weaknesses,

00:06:56.278 --> 00:06:59.039
then you can work with
people that complement you.

00:06:59.039 --> 00:07:00.833
I mean, every one of my businesses,

00:07:00.833 --> 00:07:04.578
I've had a partner who's
very anal (laughing).

00:07:04.578 --> 00:07:08.875
Martin Woodall, Todd Wagner, I
mean, incredibly anal people,

00:07:08.875 --> 00:07:11.515
perfectionists, because I'm a slob.

00:07:11.515 --> 00:07:14.601
I'm a big picture, think about
what's around the corner,

00:07:14.601 --> 00:07:17.101
how's technology going to
change things, how can I change,

00:07:17.101 --> 00:07:20.003
you know, this industry, and you know,

00:07:20.003 --> 00:07:21.109
making sure that there's somebody there

00:07:21.109 --> 00:07:23.639
to dot the I's, cross the T's and keep me

00:07:23.639 --> 00:07:27.472
in the baselines and
recognizing my weaknesses

00:07:28.603 --> 00:07:31.756
is just as important as
recognizing my strengths

00:07:31.756 --> 00:07:35.923
and my core competencies and
having the passion to do 'em.

